hermesedemobot:
[Part 1/2]
Reusable prompt — paste into any fresh Hermes session to replicate this setup.
Replace the placeholders per bot.

```
Set up Telegram webhook receiving for Hermes.

FIRST decide which branch applies:
A) A webhook link already exists (a domain with TLS + nginx route is serving
https://<your-domain>) -> go to step 2.
B) NO webhook link exists yet (no usable domain, TLS cert, or nginx route
pointing at Hermes) -> do step 1 first, THEN the rest.

Placeholders to substitute first:
DOMAIN = <your-domain> e.g. www.m3ktab.com
WEBHOOK_PATH = <path> e.g. api/telegram
BOT_TOKEN = <bot token> e.g. 8517064643:xxxx
PUBLIC_IP = <server public IP> e.g. 150.230.13.148

Step 1 — CREATE the webhook link if none exists (branch B only):
a. DNS: the DOMAIN must resolve to this server's PUBLIC_IP. Check with
`getent hosts <DOMAIN>`; if it points elsewhere or doesn't resolve, tell me
to create an A/AAAA record to PUBLIC_IP in the DNS provider, then continue.
b. Install nginx if missing; ensure it listens on 80 and 443.
c. Get a TLS cert (Let's Encrypt via certbot) for the DOMAIN, e.g.
`sudo certbot --nginx -d <DOMAIN> -d www.<DOMAIN>` (DNS must already resolve
to this box before certbot will issue).
d. Add an nginx location block to the DOMAIN's 443 `server {}` that proxies the
<WEBHOOK_PATH> to Hermes's webhook listener (default 8443) and passes the
auth headers. Back up the config, `nginx -t`, then reload. The location must
match the EXACT path Telegram will POST to (e.g. location /api/telegram):
location /api/telegram {
proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8443;
proxy_set_header Host $host;
proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
proxy_read_timeout 120s;
}
e. Verify the route reaches Hermes: after the gateway's 8443 listener is up, a
GET to https://<DOMAIN>/<WEBHOOK_PATH> should return 4xx/5xx FROM HERMES
(405/400/403), NOT a 404 from nginx. A 404 from nginx = the location block is
wrong or not applied.

Step 2 — Verify the site answers over TLS (branch A and B):
curl -I https://<DOMAIN>/ -> expect HTTP 200, nginx.

Step 3 — Add the telegram webhook settings to the ACTIVE profile's secret store
(~/.hermes/.env, or $HERMES_HOME if a profile). Do NOT put these in config.yaml
— the Telegram adapter reads them from secret scope only:
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL=https://<DOMAIN>/<WEBHOOK_PATH>
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<64-char hex, e.g. secrets.token_hex(32)>
Strip/replace any existing TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_* lines first. Never print secrets.

Step 4 — Register the webhook with Telegram, passing the secret as secret_token
so updates are signed (verify Telegram returns {"ok":true}):
POST https://api.telegram.org/bot<BOT_TOKEN>/setWebhook
url=https://<DOMAIN>/<WEBHOOK_PATH>
secret_token=<TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET>

Step 5 — Point nginx's <WEBHOOK_PATH> location to Hermes's webhook listener
(default port 8443, host 0.0.0.0): proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8443; then reload
nginx (already done in step 1 for branch B). Warn me if an existing service
already owns that path (e.g. an old receiver script on port 8080) so it doesn't
fight Hermes for updates.

Step 6 — Tell me to run `hermes gateway setup`, enable the Telegram platform, and
enter the bot token. The gateway reads TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL + TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET
from .env, starts the 8443 listener, and pulls updates.

Step 7 — Verify end-to-end:
a. curl https://api.telegram.org/bot<BOT_TOKEN>/getWebhookInfo -> url matches,
pending_update_count is low after a test message.

b. If pending_update_count > 0 or last_error_message shows a non-200, the
nginx route is broken — confirm the location block matches the exact URL
path and nginx was reloaded.
c. Send a real message to the bot and confirm the gateway receives it (check
gateway.log for the incoming update; gateway_state.json should show telegram
state "connected").

Note: webhook mode disables long-polling (getUpdates) for this bot — intentional.
A queued message that never arrives is usually the nginx route returning 404,
not a gateway problem: check getWebhookInfo for last_error_message first.

